Original commit body from 1bfce4bc36c54b765fb95c5eee5b1f145f399c6b:

`requests.post(url, data={"s": secret})` produced ZERO net_send chains while
open()/file.read/os.environ controls fired in the same run. io_primitives/
python.yaml explained it away: third-party libraries "are detected transitively
-- they ultimately call these primitives". THAT IS FALSE at hypergumbo's
analysis scope. hypergumbo analyzes the target repository, not site-packages,
so no edge reaches requests' internals and its own socket call is never seen.
The header is corrected alongside this change.

THE FIX IS NOT MORE BUILT-IN ROWS, AND MY FIRST PROPOSAL WAS WRONG. Adding
requests/httpx/urllib3 to the shipped catalogue contradicts ADR-0016 §27 -- "a
curated list of stdlib functions, NOT an unbounded set of library APIs" -- with
§300 pricing the curation cost. Owning every third-party HTTP API's surface is
exactly the maintenance burden that ADR declined. The stdlib scope stays; only
the false justification beside it is corrected.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MISSING. ADR-0017 §370 granted project-local catalogues to the
TAINT arm ("any project can define its own taint sources, sinks, and sanitizers
... with project-local entries taking precedence"), shipped as --taint-sources
and friends. It was never extended to BOUNDARIES: load_catalog(language) read
only the packaged directory and extra_catalogs: accepted only the three taint
keys. ADR-0016 predates ADR-0017 and scoped itself before that pattern existed.

NEW: --io-primitives PATH (repeatable) on io-boundaries and verify-claims, plus
extra_catalogs: io_primitives: in a claims file. Precedence mirrors the taint arm
rather than inventing a second rule -- built-in < claims-file extras < CLI flags,
later path wins, keyed on qualified name via the IoBoundaryCatalog.merge
primitive that language inheritance (scala <- java) already uses. Measured
end-to-end on the reproducer: net_send 0 -> 2 (requests.get, requests.post),
env_read/fs_read controls unchanged, overlay set disclosed on stderr so an
io-boundaries diff stays attributable.

ONE DECLARATION FEEDS BOTH ARMS. ADR-0017 §453 already made io_primitives the
single source of truth for built-in taint sinks -- every write-side primitive
auto-derives via AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP, no taint_sinks/ directory ships -- "without
a second source of truth that could drift out of sync". An overlay feeding only
the boundary arm would have re-created that drift one layer up, with the USER
paying for it by declaring requests.post twice in two schemas. Overlays are
threaded into the same derivation, grouped by declared language so a Go overlay
never seeds Python sinks: python taint sinks 113 -> 172, requests.post arriving
as zone=network, trust_level=untrusted. Additive-only (more sinks can add
findings, never delete one); non-destruction of the built-in sink set asserted.

THE FORMATS STAY SEPARATE, because only SINKS overlap. A taint source carries a
label and a sanitizer clears taint -- neither is an I/O crossing, neither has an
io_primitives counterpart -- and a sink additionally carries zone + trust level,
which `boundary` deliberately does not model. Merging the schemas would
re-conflate the axis kind ADR-0023/0027/0028/0031/0032 exist to keep apart.

THREE GUARDS keep an overlay from laundering itself into the curated
catalogue's standing: status: overlay required and status: complete REFUSED (it
asserts a provenance-backed stdlib enumeration); stdlib_modules/stdlib_prefixes
dropped so is_stdlib_module keeps answering about the actual interpreter (a
requests overlay must not relabel a PyPI package as stdlib -- that feeds the
dependency classifier and the F3 filter); and a missing or malformed path is an
error at exit 2, never a silent skip, because "no extra primitives" reads
exactly like a clean repo.

Worked example at docs/io-primitives-overlays/python-http-clients.yaml --
deliberately NOT beside the shipped catalogues, because the user owns those rows.
ADR-0016 carries the amendment.

Tests: 5768 passed; all six packages 100% in isolation.

